<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#nahuatl">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahuatl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nahuatl is a Uto-Aztecan language spoken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and other peoples of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> spread its use across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and it carried a tradition of poetry and learning. After the Spanish conquest it became the most important indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of the colonial period, used by missionaries for evangelisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahuatl.jpg" alt="Nahuatl" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine Codex, workshop of Bernardino de Sahagún · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Florentine_Codex-_Life_in_Mesoamerica_IV.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nahuatl has been spoken in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> since at least the seventh century CE. It belongs to the Nahuan branch of the Uto-Aztecan family, all of whose varieties descend from a single Proto-Nahuan language. Linguists in the 20th century largely held that Uto-Aztecan began in the southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and that early Nahuan speakers moved south into central Mexico in several waves. Jane H. Hill has proposed instead that the family began in central Mexico and spread north, a view that has met serious criticism.</p><p>The name comes from the Classical Nahuatl word nāhuatlahtōlli, 'clear language'. Many speakers call the language Mexicano, or use a term related to mācēhualli, 'commoner'. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> dominated central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the Late Postclassic period, and their influence made the variety spoken in their city a prestige language across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Nahuan languages are polysynthetic and agglutinative: roots take many prefixes and suffixes, and a single word can serve as a whole sentence. Classical Nahuatl had no case or gender, a base-20 number system, and stress on the penultimate syllable. Its speakers distinguished the language of commoners, macehuallahtolli, from that of the nobility, tecpillahtolli, whose formal oratory used parallel couplets and paired metaphors known as difrasismos.</p><p>Before the conquest, Aztec writing was pictographic. Codices served as aids to memory for genealogies, astronomical information and tribute lists, and were meant to be told rather than read. Nahuatl distinguished tlahtolli, speech, from cuicatl, song, much as prose differs from poetry. Varieties today differ considerably, and some are not mutually intelligible; the Mexican Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas recognises 30 of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Spanish arrived in 1519, Nahuatl lost its place as the leading regional language but stayed important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> communities. Colonists and missionaries introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script, and chronicles, grammars, poetry, administrative documents and codices were written in it in the 16th and 17th centuries. This Classical Nahuatl, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> variety, is among the best-documented Indigenous languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. In 1570 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> decreed that it should be the official language of the colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>.</p><p>Nahuatl supplied Spanish, and through it many other languages, with words for things of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, among them chocolate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a>, avocado, chili, coyote and axolotl. The names Mexico, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua derive from it. Its use has declined over time: in 1895 more than 5 per cent of Mexico's population spoke it, and by 2000 the figure was 1.49 per cent. About 1.7 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a> speak it today, and Huasteca Nahuatl, with over a million speakers, is the commonest variety.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nahuatl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They record <b>Nahuatl</b> poetry, advanced astronomical and calendrical knowledge, elaborate philosophical and cosmological systems, and an urban planning tradition that astonished European observers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…colonial times, tell how Hunac Ceel, head chief of Mayapan, drove Chac Xib Chac, ruler of Chichen Itza, from his city with the help of captains bearing <b>Nahuatl</b> names, an episode they place in a Katun 8 Ahau, which in the usual correlation ran from 1185 to 1204.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The law distinguished a republic of Spaniards from a republic of Indians, whose towns kept their own councils and nobles, and <b>Nahuatl</b>, Mixtec and other languages were used in local records.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At its head stood the king, called irecha in Purépecha and cazonci by the Spaniards, from a <b>Nahuatl</b> word, who ruled as the earthly representative of Curicaueri; kingship remained in the lineage of Tariácuri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its original name is unknown; Teotihuacan is the <b>Nahuatl</b> name later given to it by the Aztecs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Nahuatl</b> the word toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and the Aztecs called art and refinement toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
